### =============================
### Notification Service API Tests
### =============================
### Prerequisite: user-service must be running on port 8081.
### Notifications are created only by consuming RabbitMQ events (ride.status.changed,
### payment.completed) — there is no REST endpoint to create one directly. To generate
### real data for the read/unread/mark-as-read tests below, either run a full ride flow
### through ride-service, or publish a test message manually via the RabbitMQ management
### UI (http://localhost:15672) to the "ride.status.queue" queue, e.g.:
###   {"rideId":"r1","riderId":"<riderUserId>","driverId":null,"status":"REQUESTED","message":"Your ride has been requested","timestamp":"2026-08-09T00:00:00"}
### Run these requests sequentially from top to bottom.

### 1. Register a Rider user (via User Service)
POST http://localhost:8081/api/users/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Notif Test Rider",
  "email": "notif_rider@example.com",
  "password": "securePass123",
  "phone": "+8801755555555",
  "role": "RIDER"
}

> {%
    client.test("Rider user registration successful", function() {
        client.assert(response.status === 201, "Expected 201 Created");
    });
    client.global.set("riderUserId", response.body.id);
%}

### 2. Get notifications for a user with none yet (should be empty)
GET http://localhost:8085/api/notifications/user/{{riderUserId}}

> {%
    client.test("Empty notification list", function() {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Expected 200 OK");
        client.assert(response.body.length === 0, "Should be empty before any events are consumed");
    });
%}

### 3. Get unread notifications for a user with none yet (should be empty)
GET http://localhost:8085/api/notifications/user/{{riderUserId}}/unread

> {%
    client.test("Empty unread list", function() {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Expected 200 OK");
        client.assert(response.body.length === 0, "Should be empty before any events are consumed");
    });
%}

### 4. Mark a non-existent notification as read (should fail with 404)
PUT http://localhost:8085/api/notifications/000000000000000000000000/read

> {%
    client.test("Non-existent notification returns 404", function() {
        client.assert(response.status === 404, "Expected 404 Not Found");
    });
%}

### 5. After seeding a notification via RabbitMQ (see note above), fetch it again
GET http://localhost:8085/api/notifications/user/{{riderUserId}}

> {%
    client.test("Notification present after event consumed", function() {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Expected 200 OK");
    });
    if (response.body.length > 0) {
        client.global.set("notificationId", response.body[0].id);
    }
%}

### 6. Mark the seeded notification as read
PUT http://localhost:8085/api/notifications/{{notificationId}}/read

> {%
    client.test("Notification marked as read", function() {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Expected 200 OK");
        client.assert(response.body.isRead === true, "isRead should be true");
    });
%}
